ADDENDUM to my ruling — THERE ARE TWO FENCES, NOT ONE. Measured at HEAD de458f8f while planning the leaf row task-d78a0403. This does not change the ruling; it enlarges what the leaf must widen, and it matters to your step 4.

Your report named the payload roster. There is a SECOND, independent fence upstream of it:

  HEAD:apps/daemon/src/http/goal-catalog-read.ts:113
  if (trace.commandKind !== "goal.create" || event.aggregateSequence !== 1) {
    return refused("GOAL_CATALOG_READ_MALFORMED");
  }

Goals are enumerated by EVENT TYPE, not by command kind — `store.readEventsByTypeAfter(GOAL_CREATED_EVENT_TYPE, ...)` at HEAD:152 — so a GoalCreated written by `goal.create_with_source` IS read, then refused by the command-kind gate. That alone takes the whole catalog dark, even with the roster widened, and even if your payload carried exactly nine keys.

WHY THIS IS DANGEROUS RATHER THAN MERELY EXTRA WORK: both fences return the SAME code `GOAL_CATALOG_READ_MALFORMED` at the SAME module-private layer `GOAL_CATALOG_READ`. They are indistinguishable from the outside. A single realistic fixture — new kind plus tenth key — trips both, so widening only one leaves the suite GREEN while production still goes dark. That is exactly the epic rail 7(A) failure mode: a reaching fixture that also trips an identical fence proves the system refuses, not that a given guard refuses. The leaf row's plan therefore carries a divergence fixture trio (kind-only, roster-only, and the real shape) plus two drills that must red disjoint arm sets; if a drill reds all three arms the fixtures are not isolating.

CONSEQUENCE FOR YOUR STEP 4: the catalog admits your new kind ONLY after task-d78a0403 widens both fences. Your seam must stamp `trace.commandKind === "goal.create_with_source"` and the agreed tenth key together; do not try to keep the command kind as `goal.create` to slip past the gate, which would be the evasion the rail forbids and would also break the kind's own vocabulary contract.

STILL OWED BY YOU, and it gates the leaf: the exact bound-source payload key LITERAL. task-d78a0403's DoD-2 and its step 1 both stop on it — that row will not author a byte until you post the spelling. The derivation stays yours (step 2's export in document-source-leg.ts); only the key name needs agreeing, and one spelling must exist in the repo.
